  "moments": [
    {
      "id": "cinco-seguidas",
      "title": {
        "es": "Cinco victorias seguidas",
        "en": "Five wins in a row"
      },
      "text": {
        "es": "Steinitz mantuvo el equilibrio hasta la sexta partida, pero Lasker ganó de la 7 a la 11 y tomó una ventaja decisiva.",
        "en": "Steinitz held the balance until game 6, but Lasker won games 7 through 11 and took a decisive lead."
      },
      "round": 11
    },
    {
      "id": "reaccion-en-montreal",
      "title": {
        "es": "La reacción en Montreal",
        "en": "The fightback in Montreal"
      },
      "text": {
        "es": "Ya en Montreal, Steinitz ganó las partidas 13 y 14, pero Lasker respondió ganando la 15 y la 16.",
        "en": "In Montreal, Steinitz won games 13 and 14, but Lasker answered by winning games 15 and 16."
      },
      "round": 14
    },
    {
      "id": "nuevo-campeon",
      "title": {
        "es": "Un nuevo campeón",
        "en": "A new champion"
      },
      "text": {
        "es": "Con su victoria en la partida 19, el 26 de mayo de 1894, Lasker llegó a diez triunfos y se convirtió en el segundo campeón del mundo.",
        "en": "With his win in game 19 on 26 May 1894, Lasker reached ten wins and became the second world champion."
      },
      "round": 19
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "text": {
        "es": "Lasker quería jugar por 5.000 dólares por bando; se acordaron 3.000, pero Steinitz aceptó rebajas porque a Lasker le costaba reunir el dinero, y al final fueron 2.000 por jugador.",
        "en": "Lasker wanted $5,000 a side; $3,000 was agreed, but Steinitz accepted reductions because Lasker struggled to raise the money, and it ended at $2,000 each."
      },
      "source": {
        "label": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Chess_Championship_1894"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": {
        "es": "Se jugó en tres ciudades: las partidas 1 a 8 en Nueva York, la 9 a la 11 en Filadelfia y la 12 a la 19 en Montreal.",
        "en": "It was played in three cities: games 1–8 in New York, 9–11 in Philadelphia and 12–19 in Montreal."
      },
      "source": {
        "label": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Chess_Championship_1894"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": {
        "es": "Según el maestro Jack Peters, Lasker buscó a propósito cambiar damas pronto, porque había notado que Steinitz dudaba en las posiciones simplificadas.",
        "en": "According to master Jack Peters, Lasker deliberately sought early queen trades because he had noticed Steinitz's uncertainty in simplified positions."
      },
      "source": {
        "label": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Chess_Championship_1894"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": {
        "es": "Años después Lasker escribió sobre su rival: «Era un pensador digno de un lugar en las aulas de una universidad. Un jugador, como el mundo creía que era, no lo era».",
        "en": "Years later Lasker wrote of his rival: \"He was a thinker worthy of a seat in the halls of a University. A player, as the world believed he was, he was not.\""
      },
      "source": {
        "label": "Mark Weeks",
        "url": "https://www.mark-weeks.com/chess/z4ls$wix.htm"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": {
        "es": "En abril de 1894, en pleno match, el British Chess Magazine describía a Steinitz como dueño del «campeonato de ajedrez del mundo desde hace 28 años», contando desde su victoria sobre Anderssen en 1866.",
        "en": "In April 1894, during the match, the British Chess Magazine described Steinitz as holding \"the chess championship of the world for 28 years\", counting from his 1866 win over Anderssen."
      },
      "source": {
        "label": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilhelm_Steinitz"
      }
    }
  ],
